<section class="glasner" id="glasner">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="glasner-header">
      <p class="section-label">Glencoe's Other Wright</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">The Glasner House, 1905</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="glasner-body">
      <div class="glasner-text">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          A decade before Wright broke ground on the Booth House, he was already working in
          Glencoe. The William A. Glasner House at 850 Sheridan Road, designed in 1905, is
          Wright's earliest commission in the village — and one of the most quietly extraordinary
          houses of his career. Where the Booth House climbs vertically from its ravine, the
          Glasner House clings horizontally to the edge of one, nestled into a bluff above a
          steep wooded descent to the ravine below.
        </p>

        <p>
          Glasner was a bicycle manufacturer — the same trade as Frederick Robie, for whom Wright
          would later design the celebrated Robie House in Hyde Park. The commission called for
          a summer cottage, and Wright responded with a one-story asymmetrical design organized
          around two octagonal rooms — an octagonal library and an octagonal sewing room — with a
          living room crowned by a vaulted hipped ceiling. The octagon was a form Wright had
          explored in earlier commissions, evoking the domestic experiments of the Chauncey
          Williams and George Furbeck houses in River Forest, but here it serves a different
          purpose — anchoring the house to the irregular, picturesque topography of the site.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          "Perhaps the only work of Wright's early years that truly relates to its site."
          <cite>— Architectural historians on the Glasner House's relationship to its ravine</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          The most celebrated feature of the Glasner House is its series of casement windows,
          known as the "Tree of Life" windows. The leaded glass pattern features large iridescent
          squares in yellow-green and orange — colors that change quality depending on the light
          and the angle of view, glowing from the exterior and flooding the interior with
          warm-toned natural light. Wright called his windows "light screens," arguing that they
          dissolved the distinction between inside and outside, between the house and the world
          of nature surrounding it. At the Glasner House, that philosophy finds one of its
          earliest and most lyrical expressions.
        </p>

        <p>
          Architectural historians have consistently identified the Glasner House as a
          <strong>precursor to Fallingwater</strong>. The 1936 Bear Run masterpiece is perhaps
          Wright's most famous house, and its drama comes from the way it projects over a
          waterfall, merging structure with landscape. The Glasner House anticipates this gesture
          thirty years earlier — a house poised at the edge of a void, designed not despite its
          difficult site but because of it. The ravine is not a problem to be solved; it is the
          point.
        </p>

        <h3 class="glasner-subhead">Preservation</h3>

        <p>
          The Glasner House narrowly escaped demolition in the early 2000s, when the family
          who owned it decided the house was impractical for modern family life and a real estate
          developer moved to purchase and replace it. The Frank Lloyd Wright Building Conservancy
          intervened, locating a conservation-minded buyer in Jack Reed, who purchased the
          property in 2003 for $1.5 million and subsequently invested $2.5 million in a
          comprehensive restoration by Vinci-Hamp Architects, completed in 2011. The restoration
          included structural stabilization, the reversal of unsympathetic later modifications,
          restoration of the leaded glass windows, rehabilitation of the landscape to its
          original descent into the ravine, and the integration of new mechanical systems
          including geothermal heat pumps. Reed later donated a preservation easement protecting
          the exterior and all significant spaces of the main floor in perpetuity.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Glasner House is a private residence at 850 Sheridan Road, Glencoe, Illinois —
          not open to the public. Together with the Ravine Bluffs Development, it makes Glencoe
          the holder of the third-largest concentration of Frank Lloyd Wright structures in the
          state of Illinois.
        </p>

        <div class="glasner-specs">
          <div class="spec-item">
            <span class="spec-label">Designed</span>
            <span class="spec-value">1905</span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-item">
            <span class="spec-label">Address</span>
            <span class="spec-value">850 Sheridan Road, Glencoe</span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-item">
            <span class="spec-label">Style</span>
            <span class="spec-value">Prairie — Early Period</span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-item">
            <span class="spec-label">Restored</span>
            <span class="spec-value">2003–2011, Vinci-Hamp Architects</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="glasner-figure-stack">
        <figure class="glasner-figure">
          <div class="glasner-img-frame">
            <img
              src="/images/glasner/glasner-exterior.jpg"
              alt="The Glasner House at 850 Sheridan Road, Glencoe"
              loading="lazy"
              onerror="this.parentElement.classList.add('no-image')"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption>W. A. Glasner House, 850 Sheridan Road — Wright's first Glencoe commission</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="glasner-figure">
          <div class="glasner-img-frame">
            <img
              src="/images/glasner/glasner-windows.jpg"
              alt="The Tree of Life windows at the Glasner House"
              loading="lazy"
              onerror="this.parentElement.classList.add('no-image')"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption>The "Tree of Life" casement windows — iridescent yellow-green and orange leaded glass</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .glasner {
    background: #F0EBE3;
  }

  .glasner-header {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-lg);
  }

  .glasner-body {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 320px;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .glasner-text p {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .drop-cap::first-letter {
    font-size: 4.5rem;
    line-height: 0.8;
    float: left;
    margin-right: 0.1em;
    margin-top: 0.08em;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .pull-quote {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .pull-quote cite {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-style: normal;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .glasner-subhead {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .glasner-specs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    padding-top: var(--space-md);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .spec-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }

  .spec-label {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .spec-value {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .glasner-figure-stack {
    position: sticky;
    top: 7rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }

  .glasner-figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .glasner-img-frame {
    aspect-ratio: 4/3;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-border);
  }

  .glasner-img-frame.no-image {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .glasner-img-frame.no-image::after {
    content: "Photo coming soon";
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .glasner-img-frame.no-image img {
    display: none;
  }

  .glasner-img-frame img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .glasner-figure figcaption {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .glasner-body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .glasner-figure-stack {
      position: static;
      flex-direction: row;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .glasner-figure {
      flex: 1 1 280px;
    }
  }
</style>
